@@ -0,0 +1,6116 @@
+# ptvencoder version history
+
+Per-release notes, extracted verbatim from the `ptvencoder.c` header on 2026-07-03
+(the in-code block had grown to ~190 comment lines). **Add new release notes HERE,**
+keep only the current `PTVENCODER_VERSION` define in the source. This file is part of
+the v2 `0001` patch (additive, travels with the source to the build box).
+
+## 2.2.0-rc4 — 2.2.0-rc3 + SCTE-35 survives a large erased step back (T-453)
+
+**SCTE-35 keeps flowing after the source jumps far back in time (T-453).** cor-3, 2026-11-09 09:06 CST (rc3): the source of
+Growing_Your_Faith stepped its clock back 41523 s; the timeline erased the step correctly, but from then on the output carried
+no SCTE-35 at all (0 packets on the cue PID) and the process grew ~25-30 MB/h — every cue was held in the muxers' interleave
+buffers. One cue of the OLD content, read 33 ms before the step's window opened, was moved onto the new timeline by the
+step's offset (the rule that a section read within two video pictures of a step belongs to the new content — right for the
+-120 ms step it was made for, wrong when the overlap is the whole old timeline). It then waited for a picture that never
+came, every later cue waited behind it, and after the queue filled they went out chained behind a label 41479 s ahead. Now a
+cue read in that lag counts as new content only when its time lies within 10 s of the new content's start.
+Kit (new key vt-t453 clean on cap-gyfstep, media-v13: a GYF source capture with the step re-created): rc3 FAIL — SCTE-35
+stops at the step; rc4 PASS — 3.7 cues/s to the end.
+
+## 2.2.0-rc3 — 2.2.0-rc2 + a source clock excursion nets to zero (T-451)
+
+**A source whose clock steps away and comes back keeps its sync (T-451).** cor-3, 2026-11-07 02:50 (rc2): the source of
+Curiosity_University and of MV_2x2_Curiosity_Channels slot 1 stepped its clock back 14768 s and returned 2 s later (the same
+source did it on 11-06 03:40 too). The audio crossed first while the video went on on its old path; 300 ms past the crossing
+point the video was taken as "did not cross", so the departure went out as two single-stream steps ("labels as sent") and the
+joint return was erased — the output ended 4 h off: the single's A/V 126 s apart, the slot re-locked +14766 s without sound,
+both restarted by sync_check. On 11-06 the return had split too and the two halves happened to cancel. Now:
+- a step the input's silence does not explain (a step back beyond 2 s, or a forward step beyond the stream's own silence by
+  more than 2 s) waits up to the full window (3 s) for its partners instead of judging a partner that goes on on its old path
+  as "did not cross" after 300 ms (the video's pictures keep flowing meanwhile). A plain hole closes as before, and a partner
+  already more than 2 s past the crossing point did not cross (a video nulled 20 s while its audio flowed);
+- the audio partner of a program event must have crossed by the video's amount (an audio's return leg arriving in the window
+  of the video's departure made one "step back erased" of two opposite steps);
+- a program event takes its damage phase with it, and the phase ends at its last corrupt packet, not at the window's close
+  (a return 2 s after a damaged departure kept 2.12 s of content that was all there as a hole; live 1.88 s).
+Kit (new scenario sync_excursion; keys vt / vt-ar01 / vt-mirror sync_excursion + mv04-t451): rc2 FAIL on every shape (audio
+dead for the rest of the run / dark 8.85 s / slot 0 dark 110 s), rc3 PASS with the excursion netting to ±0.04 s. The T-431 /
+T-433 scenarios (jumpback, restartlate) are gate keys now too. Full gate vs rc1: 0 regressions (the first two rc3 builds held a
+plain 10 s gap and a 20 s video-only null 3 s for a partner — caught by the gate, fixed before release). One deliberate change:
+a video-only forward relabel with no wall silence (gate fwd_pts_jump) now waits ~1.9 s for a partner (rc2 0.2 s) — in its
+first second it is indistinguishable from an excursion whose audio leads.
+
+## 2.2.0-rc2 — 2.2.0-rc1 + a mosaic slot back from an outage longer than its input timeout keeps the hole (T-449)
+
+**A mosaic slot drops the PES tails a read timeout flushes (T-449).** cor-3 MV_2x2_Curiosity_Channels, 2026-11-06 (pre12):
+two slot sources were dark 19 h 12 m. At the slot input timeout (30 min) the failed read made mpegts hand back its pending
+PES; the slot took that tail as its last arrival, so on the return the hole was measured from the timeout and the timeline
+erased 1801 s of it as flow — the slot re-locked +1801 s, its delay ran away and its sound never came back until sync_check
+restarted the mosaic (DEAD_AUDIO_TRACK). The 2.0.0-pre5 rule that drops these tails was single-input only; it now covers
+every reopening input, and a mosaic slot logs `[PTV-REOPEN] input '…' read failed (…) — N flushed PES tails dropped`.
+Kit (new key mv04-t449/sync_gap_40: a 40 s slot outage with a 10 s slot timeout): rc1 FAIL 2/2 (9.8 s erased, slot sound
+dark 8.8 s, the other slots' sound 0.4 s gaps) → PASS 3/3, hole kept. Mosaic gate keys (kit 5d72d5b, media-v11): 39/39 PASS,
+0 regressions vs the rc baseline.
+
+## 2.2.0-rc1 — the 2.2.0 release candidate: 2.2.0-pre12 unchanged, version only
+
+**Same code as 2.2.0-pre12** (adaptive-2.2 5fc94ca504); only `PTVENCODER_VERSION` changes. What makes it the candidate:
+- **24 h canary soak of pre12** (live-transcoder + tmtg-cor-transcoder-3, 47 channels, 2026-11-05 07:46 → 11-06 07:46):
+  0 cores, 0 restart loops; single-input lip sync within ±20 ms all day; regulation as designed (WB Port / WB Int / JLTV /
+  In Trouble regulated, Heartland / Retro / Revn / Sportz switched once and stayed, TruBLU 01:45 → 01:55 switched and
+  regulated again = T-444 live).
+- **Gate on the frozen 2.2.0-rc kit** (kit28 681e5a4, tag `kit-2.2.0-rc`, media-v11): 265 keys, 260 PASS, 0 INVALID; the
+  5 FAILs are the 2 SCTE-35 "KNOWN lead-outage only" keys + 3 keys that fail identically on every binary (pre11 too).
+- **T-071 fleet parity** (`-bind_report` vs the wrapper): 286 programs identical, 6 differences all intended / fixed /
+  owner-accepted, 39 offline (autostart=false or dead source).
+
+## 2.2.0-pre12 — 2.2.0-pre11 + a wandering PCR stays regulated, the no-clock switch is reversible, steps keep subtitles on their picture (T-443, T-444, T-332)
+
+**A PCR that wanders against its arrivals is still a clock (T-443).** On cor-3 Western_Bound_Portugues switched to
+pass-through ~9 min after start ("its PCR runs ahead of the arrivals"). Measured on 3 h read-only captures of WB Int /
+Port: the packets arrive at real time (arrival − video PTS within ±0.1 s), but the PCR wanders up to ±1 s against them,
+in steps of −1.4…−9.8 ms (the log's "PCR jump −0.0 s") and +1.06 / +1.17 s. pre11 took each step as a jump and, the last
+scheduled time having passed, as an outage: it re-anchored at that packet + D, the next packets waited up to 2.3 s,
+rising — the false "runs ahead" rule. Now a step back of less than 0.1 s is jitter (the schedule goes on), and a jump
+re-anchors at arrival + D only after a real outage (arrivals stopped for 1 s or more, or a reopen); otherwise the
+schedule goes on and the drift servo follows. Replays: WB Int pre11 3 / 3 switched, 12-18 repeats, +0.4-0.6 s → 0 / 3,
+0 repeats, +0; WB Port worst wait 1.46-1.51 s → 0.74-0.76 s.
+
+**The no-clock switch is reversible (T-444, owner 2026-11-04: a glitch over a long run must not leave channels switched
+for good).** pre11 switched on the first sign and stayed switched until a restart; on cor-3 a glitch did that to two
+clock sources in a day (WB Port above; TruBLU: a third 1-2 s PCR gap inside source damage). While switched, the schedule
+keeps running on the PCR in the background; either sign (a 1 s PCR gap with data flowing, a 2 s window whose earliest
+packet would wait more than D + max(1 s, D / 2)) restarts a 10 min count. 10 min without a sign = regulated again: the
+current packet is due now and the drift servo builds the hold up to D (about 40 s at D 0.5 s, no step). One line each way:
+`[PTV-REG] inK … no clock to pace by: the input passes as it arrives (not regulated) until its PCR is a clock again for
+10 min` / `[PTV-REG] inK its PCR has been a clock for 10 min — regulated again (the hold builds up to D 0.5 s over ~1 min)`;
+the status line shows `unreg=N` while N inputs are switched. Detection stays fast (~6 s): a Lukentv source (Heartland)
+keeps switching at once and never returns (its sign recurs every 4 s). Gate key `vt-regback` (a WB Port slice with 3 PCR
+gaps, a 30 s test window): pre11 FAIL, pre12 PASS.
+
+**A subtitle / cue muxed after a stepped picture takes the step (T-332).** After a backward source step the copied DVB
+subtitles and SCTE-35 cues whose source time lies in the overlap with the old content are told apart by read order. lavf
+hands the stepped picture out up to two video packets after its PES started, so a subtitle muxed right after it was read
+first and kept the old offset: gate rich5994b `sync_bstep`, the third −120 ms step, the DVB subtitle and its cue 120 ms
+early on every run. Now a copy read in that lag that lies after the stepped picture in the mux (or has no position: a
+section) belongs to the new content. Same key: −120 ms → 0 ms.
+
+**Full gate** (kit 542d30d, media-v10, 265 runs): 260 PASS, 0 regressions against pre11, the one fix above; the 5
+non-PASS rows are the 2 SCTE-35 cues keeping their lead through an outage (by design) and 3 info keys failing on pre11 too.
+
+## 2.2.0-pre11 — 2.2.0-pre10 + the regulator paces only by a PCR that is a clock (T-441)
+
+**A PCR that is no clock is not paced by: the input passes as it arrives (as unregulated, as on pre9), said once:**
+`[PTV-REG] inK its PCR runs ahead of the arrivals … / its PCR comes only every X s … — no clock to pace by: the input
+passes as it arrives from now on (not regulated)`. What it held leaves at once.
+
+- **It runs ahead of the arrivals.** One packager's sources (Lukentv: Heartland, Retro, Revn on cor-1 / cor-3) stamp
+  each 4 s segment with 5.8 s of PCR and step back 1.8 s at the next; their PTS / DTS run exactly at real time (90 s
+  read-only capture, `fixture-media/cap-heartland-t441`). Paced by that PCR, pre10 held 1.8 s more per segment: on cor-3
+  Heartland's hold went 2.4 -> 73 s in 3 min, then the FIFO cap, 9905 of 14611 frames repeated. Rule: in three 2 s
+  windows in a row the earliest packet waits more than D + max(1 s, D / 2), each longer than the last (the drift servo
+  at its limit and losing). A hole or a jitter burst raises the wait once, then it falls. The Heartland replay switches
+  ~7 s in: 11 repeats and 0.37 s of extra delay at the start, then lip sync +0 (pre9: 0 / 0; pre10: 832 repeats and the
+  output 27.7 s behind at 89 s, growing).
+- **It comes less than once a second.** Nothing But Sportz carries one PCR per 8.3 s segment (the spec: every 0.1 s);
+  pre10 logged a false "hole 8.3 s" every 16 s. Rule: three times in 60 s the data flowed for more than 1 s with no PCR.
+  A burst's PCR jump (Fintech's +2 s) comes with PCR packets close together and does not count; nor does an outage.
+
+## 2.2.0-pre10 — 2.2.0-pre9 + every udp input regulated by default at 0.5 s, the schedule follows the source (T-439)
+
+**Regulated by default (T-439, owner 2026-11-02: "default value should be very small, as we don't want it to add too
+much delay").** No `-regulate` now means `-regulate 0.5s`; `-regulate 0` turns it off, `-regulate 8` (SV_REGULATE=8)
+stays for the bursty channels. 0.5 s covers the arrival jitter of every non-bursty production source measured over
+30 min (test-results/t439: clean sources <= 0.13 s, the glo-3 origins and WildTV 0.25-0.40 s for 99.9 % of packets,
+worst 0.50 s). A packet later than D passes as it comes, as it would unregulated, and the minute it happens in says
+so: `[PTV-REG] inK N of M PCR packets later than D in the last 60s (worst X s past due)` — a channel that prints it
+every minute (SYRIA_TV: ~1.1 s of chunky delivery) wants a larger D.
+
+**The schedule follows the source's delivery.** It was anchored at the first packet and never corrected: the glo-3
+origins' arrival wanders up to 1 s against their PCR over minutes, and the hold kept all of it as delay (TOLLY after
+a PCR glitch: 0.5 -> 4.9 s in 30 min); a source running the other way emptied it and regulation silently stopped. Now
+the earliest packet of each 10 s waits exactly D: the schedule runs -(its wait - D) / 30 s faster or slower, at most
+2 %. Measured on the built code (same binary, the servo off via the test-only `PTV_TEST_REG_NOSERVO`): a source
+0.5 % fast held 0.60 -> 2.01 s without, 0.59 -> 0.65 s with; 0.5 % slow drained to -0.94 s (every packet late)
+without, 0.35 s and none late with. It also takes the start's luck out of the hold: the first packet no longer
+decides it. With `-regulate 8` the earliest packet waits 8 s and a gap up to 8 s is covered, as before.
+
+**The FIFO grows as it fills** (from 2048 datagrams to the D + 10 s cap): a 0.5 s hold costs ~2.7 MB per input,
+not ~26 MB.
+
+**A mosaic slot drops the pre-gap tail picture by its dry spell, not a fixed 1 s.** After a hole, the slot's last pre-gap
+PES completes with post-gap bytes and decodes as a stale picture (T-350). The tail rule dropped it only when it was more
+than 1 s late. A tail is late by the dry spell less a picture. A 2.000 s hole played on its PCR (regulated by default)
+put it at 1.000 or 1.040 s, depending on the tick phase, and at 1.000 s it was shown. That stale picture re-armed the
+slot's sound bound (T-411): the returning sound parked ~0.7 s, the slot's audio PID went dark, and the mux held the whole
+mosaic for 0.9-1.0 s (gate key `mv04/sync_flapping`, 6 of 18 runs). pre9 always put it at 1.040 s, because its input saw
+the 2.08 s wire gap: 0 of 18. A tail late by more than half the dry spell is now dropped. Same key: 18 of 18 PASS,
+worst output stall 182-229 ms (pre9 141-268 ms).
+
+**The regulator follows a PCR that moves PID.** It locked onto the first PID carrying a PCR. When a provider moves the
+PCR with its video (the gate's `sync_vpidmove_30`: 0x100 -> 0x1e1 at 30 s), no PCR came on the locked PID, the
+schedule stopped, and the input ran unregulated from the move on. The gate key failed with 3 flashes without their
+beep (38.4-40.6 s), reproducibly. Now, once the locked PID has carried no PCR for 1 s, a PID that carries one takes
+over, logged as `[PTV-REG] inK PCR moved: pid A -> B`. Its first PCR continues the schedule on the same clock.
+Measured: the schedule continues at 0.50 s across the move, and the key passes 3 of 3.
+
+## 2.2.0-pre9 — 2.2.0-pre8 + lip-sync reading after a video gap, mosaic start-up, burst reports everywhere, regulator holes (T-428, T-435, T-436, T-437, T-438)
+
+**The single-input `lipsync=` reading ignores a held picture (T-428).** A short video-only gap repeats the last picture,
+and the reading's 30 s video average took every repeat in: after a 3 s gap it read +105…+144 ms, decaying over minutes,
+while the content was in sync (ruler 0.0 ms). It now applies the mosaic's rule: a picture held for 0.5 s or more stops
+feeding the reading, which re-seeds 1 s after pictures move again. The same scenario reads +0 ms at every status line.
+On a live Stingray capture with 5 s holes, replayed at its recorded arrival times, pre8 read +229…+439 ms after the
+holes; pre9 reads +0…+2 ms. Full gate (kit e45fecd, media-v7): 251 runs, 241 PASS, 0 regressions against pre7.
+
+**A mosaic no longer starts its slots seconds deep after a slow set-up (T-435).** Nothing reads the slots between the
+mosaic start and the running pipeline. On cor-3 the CUDA device took 5.2 s and the six NVENC opens about 3.2 s. In those
+~8.7 s the ~5 s udp fifos of the Curiosity 2x2 overran, and each slot began on stale content followed by a 4–5 s hole.
+The hole then became the slot's depth: a freeze of its length, every later picture that much deep, and a 4.3 s re-lock
+on air at 76 s. The slots' decoded queues also held about 0.9 GB more memory (2.3 GB against 1.4 GB). The CUDA device is
+now created on its own thread before the inputs open (`-init_hw_device cuda…`). The mosaic start waits for it while live
+slots keep reading and dropping, so only the ~3.5 s output set-up stays unread. Local repro with cor-3's timing scaled to
+the fifo: overrun lines 2246 → 8, the −6.3 s start-up hole gone, slot queue 54 → 26 pictures, RSS 270 → 205 MB (the
+no-delay baseline is 193–211 MB). A single input takes the device at the old point and only gains the overlap. The log
+line now reads `CUDA device N (… created in X s while the inputs opened)`.
+
+**A regulated input reports its bursts (T-436).** With `-regulate` the demuxer reads an already-paced stream, so
+`[PTV-BURSTY]` went quiet on exactly the channels that burst. The regulator now measures arrivals at the socket and
+prints, per minute with a stall: `[PTV-BURSTY] inK N stalls >=1.5s in the last 60s (worst X s) at the input — regulated:
+A-B s held (D d s)`. A low end near 0 means D barely covers the gaps.
+
+**A mosaic slot reports its bursts (T-438).** Each slot's demuxer measured its arrival stalls, but the per-minute line was
+printed only for a single live input, so an unregulated mosaic gave no burst report at all. A slot now prints
+`[PTV-BURSTY] inK N stalls >=1.5s in the last 60s (worst X s) — mosaic slot`. The single-input tip to set `PTV_PREROLL_MS`,
+which a slot with 3 or more stalls in a minute used to print, is no longer printed for mosaic slots. Kit mv25 with slot 0
+delivered in 6 s bursts: one slot line (`in0 11 stalls … worst 6.0s`) instead of the single-input tip; a clean mosaic
+prints no burst line. With T-436 every input now reports its bursts: single, mosaic slot, regulated or not.
+
+**The regulator keeps a real-time hole on its schedule (T-437).** An origin that skips a segment stops sending for ~5 s
+and resumes with a PCR jump of the same length (glo-3 SOFAST). The regulator took every PCR step for a restart or a loop
+and continued its schedule, which ate the hold at each hole and ran it dry at every second one. A forward jump that
+comes with an arrival stop of the same length now keeps the PCR schedule, logged `[PTV-REG] inK hole X s … kept on its
+schedule`. On the live Stingray replay, the hold after each hole is now 8.1–8.2 s; before it was 3.0 / 8.0 / 2.4 / 8.0 s,
+dropping to −2.2 s.
+
+Test-only hooks: `PTV_TEST_MV_SETUP_S` (sleep after the mosaic start, nobody reading) and `PTV_TEST_HWDEV_S` (the CUDA
+device creation takes N s more).
+
+## 2.2.0-pre8 — 2.2.0-pre7 + a regulated mosaic waits for its slots (T-434)
+
+**Mosaics with `-regulate D`: the `-slot_wait` clock starts D later (T-434).** The regulator holds every input back by D,
+so no slot can go live before D + its probe (~9 s at D = 8). Until pre7 the 3 s `-slot_wait` had always expired by then,
+and the mosaic started the moment the first slots went live. A slot that went live a moment later was starved for 4–8
+ticks at the start, then ran 160–320 ms deep; a minute in, the T-422 rule shed one tick per 10 s, so that slot's pictures
+drifted against its sound on air. Now the wait is `-slot_wait` + D (`[PTV-OUT] slot_wait 11 s expired` names the
+total when a slot is missing). Mosaics without `-regulate`, and single-input channels, are unchanged.
+
+Measured on the kit (25 fps RAV mosaic, `-regulate 8`, `sync_gap_20`, kit28 0b408b9): pre6 / pre7 — the mosaic starts with 3 of
+4 slots live, the late slot has 8 startup holds, then its offset runs to −120…−200 ms, CROSS-SLOT FAIL; the same runs
